> Approach
> Our analysis integrates findings from the climate change impacts
> literature on corals, mangroves, seaports, and fisheries and mariculture—
> four sectors for which economic impact assessment studies
> have been conducted at a sufficiently detailed spatial scale to estimate
> country-level or subnational impacts. Our process harmonizes information
> from six main disparate studies into a framework that enables
> the quantification of the ocean-based economic damages associated
> with incremental warming, a necessary requirement to estimate the
> SCC (Fig. 2). In these sectoral impact studies, climate variables such
> as sea surface temperatures (annual mean and during the warmest
> month), maritime extreme events, and coastal flooding are empirically
> linked to impacts on blue capital and statistically linked to global
> mean temperature.
> Our valuation of climate-induced damages to blue natural capital
> encompasses impacts on human wellbeing that are directly and indirectly
> reflected in markets. Market values are relatively straightforward,
> encompassing monetary losses such as decreased fisheries revenue or
> diminished trade. Non-market values include human health and nutrition
> from fisheries, coastal protection, recreational opportunities, as
> well as non-use values, generally termed existence value, that reflect
> the inherent worth that humans ascribe to ecosystems and biodiversity.
> We translate the projected changes in all blue capital categories under
> various climate change scenarios into welfare losses, and quantify these
> into monetary equivalents, allowing for a comprehensive analysis of
> ocean climate damages.
> To account for market-related damages within our framework,
> we adopt a damage function approach commonly used in IAMs.
> This method uses stylized functions that relate the projections of
> country-level market loss to increases in global mean surface temperature
> relative to pre-industrial levels. Non-market values are incorporated
> from a natural capital perspective, meaning the climate-induced
> impacts on the stock of blue natural capital in each country (measured
> as mangroves and coral area coverage) are assigned a monetary
> value equivalent to the welfare benefits provided. We use ‘benefit
> function’ to describe the relationship that maps ecosystem stock
> into a flow of benefits. These two pathways—the damage function
> and the natural capital approach—were integrated into RICE50+14,
> a computational model that simulates the dynamics of countries’
> economies under the influence of climate change. This integration
> resulted in a new version of the model, referred to as the ‘blue’
> RICE50+ model, which specifically incorporates blue capital and is
> capable of calculating the SCC for the oceans. Details are provided
> in the Methods.
