> The economic damages of climate change to the
> oceans
> We present the impacts on ocean-based welfare under a scenario representing
> a middle-of-the-road socioeconomic development path
> (SSP2) that, under a no-policy scenario, leads to an average temperature
> increase of ~3.6 °C by the end of the century over pre-industrial
> levels. Emissions and temperatures are determined endogenously
> by RICE50+, rather than by pairing SSP2 with an exogenous forcing
> pathway (for example, Representative Concentration Pathway (RCP)
> 4.5). We explore alternative socioeconomic and climate scenarios in
> the Supplementary Information and Extended Data Fig. 1.
> The annual country-level damage trajectories from 2020 to
> 2100 (Fig. 3a) vary across market use, non-market use and non-use
> values. Damages increase over time as temperatures increase and
> the size of economies grows. Overall, market use damages are the
> largest in absolute terms, totalling global annual losses of US$1.66
> trillion in 2100, followed by damages in non-use values amounting
> to US$224 billion and non-market use values adding up to $182 billion
> in annual losses.
> Non-market damages are primarily driven by the health impacts
> of reduced nutrition availability from fisheries and mariculture, access
> to which substantially impacts mortality and morbidity, especially
> in lower-income countries. Non-market use value losses from corals
> and mangroves, which provide important coastal protection services,
> are also large. The largest share of damages in non-use values comes
> from the loss of mangroves in India, where the impact on mangroves
> is substantial, and the local population benefiting from these non-use
> values is also substantial.
> One unit of loss in market-equivalent value is not equivalent to one
> unit of loss in non-market benefits, because market and non-market
> values are not perfect substitutes (Fig. 3b). In terms of the substitutability
> parameter (θ), we use a conservative elasticity of θ = 0.21 based
