> The blue Social Cost of Carbon
> To calculate the blue SCC, we first run the model under a reference
> scenario with climate impacts and then a second time with an additional
> megatonne pulse of CO2 in 2020. We aggregate the different impacts
> and combine the economic and welfare effects through a utility function
> with decreasing marginal utility with income. We normalize the
> results per tonne of CO2, computing the net present value of future
> impact streams using an endogenous discount factor (Methods). We
> keep the baseline model parameters as the central specification, and
> explore different values as sensitivity (Supplementary Table 6).
> We find that the 2020 blue SCC is US$48 per tonne of CO2 (Fig. 4a)
> (global average, without equity weights). For a pulse of CO2 in 2030, the
> blue SCC is US$72 (all values are expressed in 2020 US dollars). Roughly
> half of the blue SCC can be attributed to non-market-related losses
> from fisheries and mariculture (namely their health costs), with the
> other half stemming from corals and mangroves. Notably, the welfare
> impacts from changes to the market use values of ports are small. This
> does not imply that the accumulated damages from these sources are
> insignificant, but rather reflects the essential welfare role of oceans’
> non-market use and non-use services given their limited substitutability
> (Fig. 3) as well as their typology and geographical distribution.
> For example, health impacts are the largest component of the blue SCC
> because they have a high economic value, monetized using a globally
> uniform ‘value of a statistical life’ (VSL).
> To quantify uncertainty, we use a Monte Carlo approach to
> calculate blue SCC, with 10,000 combinations of parameter values
> (Extended Data Fig. 2). This allows us to quantify and partition uncertainty
> associated with the damage functions, ecosystem services
> benefit functions, the degree of substitutability between market,
> non-market use and non-use values, as well as climate sensitivity, the
> pure rate of time preference, and the elasticity of the marginal utility
> of consumption. Overall, the interquartile range (25th to 75th percentile)
> of the blue SCC is estimated to be between US$38 and US$70 per
> tCO2. The most important driver of uncertainty in the blue SCC is the
> elasticity of substitution between market and non-market values in the
> utility function (Extended Data Fig. 2). The SCC is also very sensitive to
> parameters governing the non-use value of corals and the non-market
> use value of fisheries. We also compute the blue SCC across different
> SSPs, finding a skewed range between US$37 (SSP5) and US$77 (SSP3)
> per tCO2 (Supplementary Fig. 25).
> The blue SCC is comparable to other major sector-specific
> 2020-SCC estimates (for example, US$84 for agriculture, US$9 for
> energy, US$90 for mortality (as given by the Greenhouse Gas Impact
> Value Estimator (GIVE) model16) or US$4 for agriculture, −US$3 for
> energy, US$39 for labour, US$3 for coastal damages due to sea-level rise
> and US$146 for mortality as given by the Data-driven Spatial Climate
> Impact Model (DSCIM)17–19, both used by the 2023 US Environmental
> Protection Agency (EPA) report11), recognizing differences in model
> structure, socioeconomic scenarios and preference parameters. To
> be able to compare our SCC values, we recalibrate the pure rate of
> time preference and elasticity of marginal utility of consumption to
> be consistent with near-term consumption discount rates of 1.5%,
> 2%, 2.5% and 3%, ensuring comparability with the GIVE model16. The
> resulting estimates are US$305, US$168, US$96 and US$64 (US$2020)
> per tCO2, respectively (Extended Data Fig. 3). For a rate of 2% per year,
> the consensus value among experts20, the blue SCC would imply an
> increase of GIVE SCC for SSP2 (US$158 (US$2020) per tCO2) by ~106%.
> Discussion and conclusions
> There has been a long-standing need to integrate marine ecological
> responses to climate change into economic metrics that inform policymaking.
> Although existing IAMs typically simulate ocean carbon
> uptake dynamics, they do not explicitly model damages to ocean
> ecosystems or ocean-based sectors. Some work has explored climate
> damages linked to ocean dynamics (for example, Atlantic Meridional
> Overturning Circulation (AMOC) slowdown21,22). However, this is the
> first estimate that comprehensively integrates climate damages to a
> range of ocean-based ecological and economic systems into the SCC
> framework. Moreover, although recent econometric work implicitly
> captures climate impacts on market sectors in regional GDP projections,
> our approach disentangles sector-specific pathways by explicitly
> modelling market damages from blue capital degradation. The
> distribution of ocean-based welfare impacts is highly unequal across
> the world, with islands and small economies being disproportionately
> affected, a pattern also observed for terrestrial-based ecosystems23.
> The blue SCC presented here implies a substantial increase relative
> to the SCC obtained when running the standard RICE50+ model
> without ocean-based damages. Specifically, our results indicate that
> including ocean-based welfare would almost double the standard
> SCC derived from the same IAM (US$51 per tCO2, in the central case;
> Extended Data Fig. 3). When we calibrate our model to the near-term 2%
> discount rate used in recent SCC estimates from the US EPA (applied in
> US policy as of 202411) our result (US$168) represents a 106% increase
> in GIVE’s SCC under SSP2. However, this comparison should be interpreted
> with caution (Supplementary Information). This increase is also
> consistent with trends in SCC estimates, which have historically risen
> as new scientific knowledge is incorporated24. The blue SCC responds
