> to numerous calls to update the scientific foundations underlying
> current SCC estimates25,26, many of which explicitly call for more comprehensive
> ocean-based assessments10,11,13,23. Our estimate expands on
> the existing estimates by incorporating several ocean-based damages
> that the US EPA acknowledges as important but has not yet included in
> SCC calculations, such as impacts on fisheries and mariculture, tourism,
> recreation and aesthetic values. The latest SCC report from the
> US National Academy of Sciences recommended the inclusion of an
> ocean chemistry module, a step toward accounting for ocean-based
> damages driven by indicators that are not closely coupled with temperature.
> Our analytical framework can be expanded to meet this goal.
> Blue SCC should be conceptually understood as a component of the
> total SCC, which includes all climate damages. However, because most
> current estimates omit ocean-based damages entirely, our results can
> be interpreted as the degree to which existing SCC estimates may be
> underestimating the true social cost of carbon emissions.
> Diverse climate drivers underlie the biophysical impacts on each
> type of blue capital analysed in this study. We standardize these impacts
> by rescaling them to global mean surface temperature for ease of
> implementation in IAMs, including RICE50+, but the underlying science
> is far more complex. For coral reefs, the estimates are driven by
> localized changes in sea surface temperature27; for mangroves, the
> key driver is the sea surface temperature of the hottest month28. Seaport
> infrastructure is influenced by a combination of pluvial, riverine
> and coastal flooding, as well as cyclones and other extreme weather
> events29. In the case of fisheries and mariculture, our approach relies
> on a process-based model of marine ecosystems—specifically, the
> dynamic bioclimate envelope model—which incorporates sea surface
> temperature, sea bottom temperature, coastal upwelling, salinity and
> other environmental factors30,31. Future implementations can retain
> these sector-specific local drivers and link them to IAMs via spatial
> patterns; the GMST rescaling used here serves as a common baseline.
> Although based on the best global knowledge, our analysis could be
> further refined to disentangle ocean drivers. For instance, increases in
> sea surface temperature contribute to coral bleaching by disrupting the
> symbiotic relationship between corals and algae. Simultaneously, ocean
> acidification impacts them by reducing their ability to form and maintain
> their structures32. These two drivers are often correlated in climate
> projections33, but disentangling their specific effects could be valuable
> for exploring alternative scenarios where mitigation policies might
> target one but not the other. For example, solar radiation management
> could reduce sea surface temperature without addressing acidification.
> The role of ecosystem services in supporting society and
