> policymaking15. Using the latest empirical evidence on substitution
> elasticities—which captures the limited potential to replace
> natural services with human-made alternatives34—and employing
> welfare functions consistent with dual discounting20,35 and previous
> IAMs36,37, we find that the non-market use and non-use values
> of ocean-based ecosystems are the largest determinants of welfare
> losses from climate change, as reflected in the blue SCC. This metric
> can also strengthen complementary efforts to assess ocean wealth.
> For example, Bertram and colleagues38 estimate ocean value by applying
> existing SCCs to the carbon sequestration of coastal ecosystems;
> incorporating a blue SCC would capture both carbon-storage benefits
> and climate damages to marine ecosystems, yielding a more complete
> ocean valuation.
> Future work should broaden blue capital coverage and benefit
> types. For instance, our fisheries values currently include only economic
> revenue and nutrition. However, for many communities, being
> ‘people of the sea’ and practising traditional fishing methods is central
> to their cultural identity—something that cannot be captured by the
> utility approach alone39,40. Moreover, future estimates should incorporate
> additional types of blue capital, such as deep-sea ecosystems,
> which provide both use and non-use values41. These contributions are
> largely unaccounted for in the SCC and, as a result, are often overlooked
> in regulatory analyses that determine climate outcomes.
> As the ocean takes a central role in climate policy, from carbon
> sequestration to broader interventions42,43, it is crucial to address both
> the impacts of policy actions and the direct effects of climate change on
> ocean ecosystems. Only by integrating these perspectives can we fully
> understand the challenges and opportunities of preserving, and where
> possible restoring, our blue capital—a critical step towards achieving
> sustainable ocean stewardship during the United Nations Decade of
> Ocean Science for Sustainable Development. With its potential for
> practical applications in regulatory analysis around the globe, an estimate
> of a blue SCC is one tool for synthesizing the global consequences
> detailed in the extensive literature on climate change impacts on the
> ocean. It also aids in designing environmental policies that account for
> the economic value of marine ecosystems.
