> Methods
> Analytical framework
> To quantify the blue SCC, we have developed an analytical framework
> designed to systematically integrate projections from biophysical and
> economic valuation studies available in the peer-reviewed literature.
> We conducted a structured literature search focused broadly on studies
> quantifying climate-induced impacts on ocean-based assets or marine
> ecosystems, with explicit consideration for those that provided corresponding
> economic welfare valuations—either directly or through
> supplementary economic assessments or meta-analyses. Our search
> resulted in four sufficiently developed sectors—corals, mangroves,
> fisheries and mariculture, and seaports—that fulfilled two key criteria:
> (1) availability of geographically explicit biophysical projections at the
> national or subnational scale and (2) associated economic valuations,
> enabling welfare impact estimation at comparable spatial resolutions.
> Our integrative approach required matching two distinct methodological
> components: the biophysical impact functions and the
> economic valuation functions. The biophysical component captures
> climate-driven changes in asset availability (for example, mangrove
> coverage, coral reef health, fisheries productivity, port functionality)
> derived from peer-reviewed climate impact modelling studies. The
> economic component translates these biophysical changes into welfare
> impacts using valuation methods appropriate for different categories
> of value: market use, non-market use and non-use. For studies already
> encompassing integrated biophysical and economic results, such as fisheries
> revenue impacts due to climate change or economic value-at-risk
> resulting from increased seaport downtime, we directly processed these
> datasets to generate reduced-form damage functions linking global
> mean surface temperature changes to country-level economic welfare
> impacts. For asset–value combinations requiring integration from multiple
> studies, we first constructed biophysical damage functions linking
> global temperature to physical impacts such as coral and mangrove area
> reductions. Subsequently, we developed simplified benefit functions
> directly relating these biophysical changes (for example, ecosystem
> area) to welfare benefits, adjusting for projected increases in income.
> The resulting damage functions facilitate integration into the
> modified IAM, RICE50+. This compatibility enables consistent and
> flexible scenario analysis under various SSPs, supporting robust exploration
> of future climate-economic outcomes.
> An essential feature of our analytical framework is its explicit
> consideration of the multiple dimensions of welfare impacts from
> ocean-based climate damages. We distinguish among three value types:
> • Market values, where welfare impacts correspond directly to
> observable market prices, such as fishery revenues and maritime
> trade through ports;
> • Non-market use values, representing benefits from direct
> ecosystem service use without explicit market pricing, such as
> nutritional health derived from fisheries or coastal protection
> from mangroves;
> • Non-use values, reflecting welfare impacts stemming from the
> intrinsic existence of ecosystems independent of direct human
> use, such as biodiversity preservation in coral reefs.
> Not all ocean-based assets exhibit all value categories due to either
> theoretical constraints or data limitations. For instance, non-use values
> are conceptually irrelevant for seaports, and non-use values for
> fisheries, although theoretically plausible, lack sufficient empirical
> quantification at the required spatial resolution. A detailed overview
> on how the valuation categories were implemented in RICE50+ for each
> sector is provided in Table 1, which clearly delineates the mechanism
> of inclusion.
> RICE50+ model structure
> To calculate the social cost of carbon for ocean-based welfare, we use
> an IAM that links the increase of a marginal tonne of CO2 emissions to
> a series of interconnected systems. The approach begins with a carbon
> cycle model that describes the atmospheric response to additional
> emissions. The resulting changes are fed into a climate model (an implementation
> of the FaIR model49), which simulates the corresponding
> temperature effects. Finally, these climatic changes are assessed for
> their impacts on ocean-based welfare, capturing the socioeconomic
> consequences of alterations in corals, mangroves, fisheries and ports.
> For this analysis, we utilize the RICE50+ model (v2.6.0)14, an
> open-source coupled climate-economy model that extends the
> well-known Regional Integrated Climate-Economy (RICE) model50.
> RICE50+ represents a dynamic framework that divides the world into
> more than 50 regions (58 in its standard version; here we run it at the
> country level with 155 countries), allowing for region-specific assessments
> of the costs and benefits of carbon emissions. The impact of
> climate change on natural capital from changes in terrestrial vegetation
> cover has been quantified recently with this model23. We
> have enhanced this model to include the latest ecological and economic
> findings on the impacts of climate change on blue capital—the
> natural and human-made assets related to the ocean—as shown in
> Supplementary Fig. 1.
> We modified the utility function (which, in the standard version,
> depends only on the level of consumption Cc,t in country c at year t) to
> account for use and non-use values of four different ocean-based systems:
> corals, mangroves, seaports, and fisheries and mariculture. We
> modified the utility function to take the following form (for simplicity,
> we omit the population here, but all values are expressed in per capita
> terms of a representative agent in each country):
