> and bvc
> rescale
> the high-resolution findings from ref. 28 to the average
> country-level value of mangroves using country-level GDP per capita.
> Therefore, the impact of climate change in these non-market values is
> only through reduced area, which would remain constant at today’s
> values if temperature did not change. Although, in theory, the marginal
> value of ecosystems may also rise as they become scarcer, we do not
> incorporate this effect here due to data limitations.
> The market damage parameters λb
> n, c for corals and mangroves are
> estimated by running two sets of simulations. First, we calculate the
> GDP contribution of corals and mangroves by multiplying their shadow
> price pb, v
> c, t by the area, and assuming no climate-induced damages to
> their areas. We then calculate the contribution under a scenario with
> climate damages. The difference between these two scenarios, as a
> percentage of GDP, is fitted to the global mean surface temperature
> using a linear function for corals, and a quadratic function for mangroves,
> following the functional forms in their respective studies of
> origin. This λb
> n, c represents a real market output loss as used in the IAM’s
> damage function—not a theoretical correction to GDP. Although our
> method uses shadow prices and is conceptually related to
> wealth-accounting frameworks, we do not compute net present values
> or deduct capital loss from national income. Instead, we keep ecosystem
> stocks in physical terms over time and compute their evolving
> contribution to market, non-market and non-use values each year.
> The non-market use values from fisheries and mariculture were
> derived from the analysis by Cheung and colleagues31 of the impacts of
> climate change on nutrient availability from fisheries and mariculture
> at the country level. We combine these projections with epidemiological
> studies on the dose–response relationships between several health
> outcomes and the intake of omega-3, calcium, iron and protein—the
> same nutrients analysed in ref. 31. With that, we project changes in
> mortality rates for each country based on climate impacts on these
> nutrients, considering each country’s level of dependence on fisheries,
> while allowing for a conservative assumption that a substantial
> portion of fish-derived nutrients can be replaced by other sources. Our
> preferred specification is to monetize these impacts using a globally
> uniform VSL. This is derived from the US EPA estimate of US$7.4 million,
> rescaled from US to global GDP per capita, for a 2020 value of US$2.15
> million, to reflect the average global GDP per capita level54. Over time,
> the VSL grows with the global average GDP per capita growth rate.
> The coefficients λfisheries
> 1, c , which represent the market value losses
> from fisheries, are obtained from a bioeconomic model by Free and
> colleagues55, which quantifies catch and profit levels at the country
> level for 60% of global fisheries under different management practices
> and climate impact scenarios. To provide a conservative estimate, we
> assume a full adaptation scenario in which maximum sustainable yield
> is maintained, allowing us to explore how profits under alternative
> climate scenarios diverge from those under the less climate-impacted
> RCP 2.6 scenario. The revenue loss at the regional level is then adjusted
> by a multiplier obtained from ref. 56, which reflects the broader economic
> impact of fisheries losses on related activities and income.
> Finally, coefficients λports
> 1, c , which account for market impacts from
> climate damages to ports, were obtained from an analysis performed
> by Verschuur and colleagues29, which estimated potential damages to
> ports due to cyclonic winds, different types of flooding (coastal, fluvial
> and pluvial) and marine extremes (extreme waves, overtopping, heat
> and non-cyclonic wind) under climate change scenarios. The damages
> include direct physical damage to port infrastructure, representing a
> financial loss due to the costs of rebuilding or repairing these infrastructures;
> operational downtime caused by such damages, leading
> to a decrease in revenue for port operators; and the impact on maritime
> trade activities for countries reliant on these ports. The damage estimates
> are obtained by fitting a linear regression to the change in damages
> to ports (as a percentage of GDP) as a function of global mean
> surface temperature.
> Details on all these components are provided in the
> Supplementary Information.
> Model simulations to estimate the blue SCC
> Based on the blue RICE50+ model output, we compute the SCC from
> oceans and separate it according to the different categories and types
> of ocean services Vb, v
> c, t , using the nested utility function (equation (1))
> and a country-level intertemporal welfare (Wc) function for each country
> as discounted utility summed over the population, which is the
> objective function for each region in the RICE50+ model:
> Wc =
> 2300
> Σ
> t=2020
> popc,t × (1 + δ)−(t−2020) × Utilityc,t (6)
> where popc,t represents the population in country c at time t, and δ is
> the pure rate of time preference. Note that in the RICE50+ framework,
> the model can be run in a global cooperative or non-cooperative mode.
> Here we use the latter, running each country separately under the different
> scenarios. Because, here, we do not implement global optimization
> scenarios, there is no issue of inequality considerations between countries,
> and we also do not apply explicit equity weighting57 to take into
> account global differences in income levels. This allows for a uniform
> and unique global value for the SCC without adding further value judgements,
> notably on inequity aversion between countries57. Accordingly,
> we aggregate population-weighted country utilities converted into
> monetary terms and apply discounting as described in equation (9).
> We ran the model once (all variables of this run are denoted without
> a hat) and then a second time adding one additional pulse of one
> megatonne of CO2 of emissions in 2020 (variables denoted by a hat).
> The pulse was added upon a future emissions pathway result of running
> the model under socioeconomic variables consistent with SSP2 and a
> no-policy scenario where the global mean temperature increase
> reaches 3.5 °C by the end of the century, equivalent to 6.8 W m−2 of
> forcing). We first computed the monetary-equivalent per capita utility
> impact at any time t and in country c by computing for each combination
> of b and v, that is, the difference in utility due to a change only due
> to impacts on Vb, v
> c, t , whereas all other variables are kept at levels of the
